# rules: kotlin-credential-is-a-literal
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-798.kt
# from: CWE-798 (Use of Hard-coded Credentials), CWE-259, CWE-321
#
# An Android app ships to a device the attacker owns. Unzipping the APK and
# grepping the string pool is the whole of the work, so a key in a `const val`
# is a key everybody who installed the app already has, and rotating it means
# shipping a release and waiting.
#
# Almost every property this rule is about has a modifier in front of it -
# `private`, `const`, `internal`, `@JvmField` - which is the whole reason it
# could not be written before xen0bit/pwrq#55: Kotlin collects them into a node
# at the head of the declaration, and a pattern that wrote none of them anchored
# its first child past it and matched only the handful of properties nobody
# marked.
#
# The name is what makes the rule look, and the *value* is what makes it
# report. That order matters, and both the Java and the C# passes learned it
# the same way: a credential-shaped name holding something that is not a
# credential is the commonest false positive there is, and there are four kinds
# of it.
#
#   "password"                a keyword in a table of them
#   "API_KEY"                 the name of an environment variable
#   "com.example.apiKey"      the name of a property
#   "${auth.token}"           a placeholder, which is the fix
#
# All four are letters and separators, and a credential is not: `hunter2`,
# `s3cr3t-admin-2019` and an API key all have digits in them, because anything
# people actually use as a secret does. So the value has to hold a digit, and
# it has to not be a placeholder, and it has to not be the name of somewhere
# the value lives.
#
# Three more exclusions are worth naming. The empty string is a local database
# with no password and reporting it teaches people to skip the rule; a URL is
# not a credential however secret the host is, which keeps
# `"https://api.example.com"` out of a rule whose word list contains `auth`;
# and a regular expression is not one either. OkHttp's MediaType parser holds
#
#     private const val TOKEN = "([a-zA-Z0-9-!#$%&'*+.^_`{|}~]+)"
#
# which is a credential-shaped name over a pattern, and it is the fifth kind of
# false positive this family of rules keeps finding. A character class, a
# group or a backslash escape is what a pattern has and a password does not.

["val $N = $V", "val $N: $T = $V", "var $N = $V", "var $N: $T = $V"] as $props
| "(?i)passw|secret|^.*api[_-]?key|token|credential|private[_-]?key|passphrase" as $namesASecret
| "^\"" as $isALiteral
| "[0-9]" as $hasADigit
| "\\$\\{|%[sd]|^\"\\s*\"$" as $isAPlaceholder
| "^\"[a-z]+://" as $isAUrl
| "[\\[\\]]|\\(\\?|\\\\\\\\|\\.\\*|\\.\\+" as $isARegex
| scan_ast(["*.kt", "*.kts"]; $props) as $all
| ( $all | of($props)
    | where_capture("N"; $namesASecret)
    | where_capture("V"; $isALiteral)
    | where_capture("V"; $hasADigit)
    | where_capture_not("V"; $isAPlaceholder)
    | where_capture_not("V"; $isAUrl)
    | where_capture_not("V"; $isARegex)
    | focus("V") )
| finding("kotlin-credential-is-a-literal";
    "$N is compiled into the app, so everyone who installed it has this value "
    + "and changing it means shipping a release")
| report
